00:12:01.867 --> 00:12:04.117
"about being overlooked
by men and self-hatred,

00:12:04.117 --> 00:12:05.917
"but rather through a post-ironic,

00:12:05.917 --> 00:12:08.977
"self-conscious embrace of
aesthetic feminine toxicity.

00:12:08.977 --> 00:12:12.360
"It's not femcel for these
women, it's femcel-core."

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.270
For example, one 19-year-old
Tumblr user named Hannah

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:18.667
who regularly uses the
hashtag #femcel said,

00:12:18.667 --> 00:12:19.987
"I just thought the word was funny

00:12:19.987 --> 00:12:21.997
"and maybe even a little shocking.

00:12:21.997 --> 00:12:23.707
"I knew it would get people's attention.

00:12:23.707 --> 00:12:25.237
"Most of my posts are ironic.

00:12:25.237 --> 00:12:26.227
"I've been in a relationship

00:12:26.227 --> 00:12:28.410
"with my boyfriend for two years."

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:32.160
Similarly, a 22-year-old
TikTokker named Alexandra Carmona

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:35.520
admitted her female
manipulator content is ironic.

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:36.517
She told Insider that,

00:12:36.517 --> 00:12:38.857
"It's not about promoting
actual manipulation.

00:12:38.857 --> 00:12:40.357
"It's about calling ourselves out

00:12:40.357 --> 00:12:43.440
"and coping with her own issues
by making a joke out of it."

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:46.350
So aesthetically I've
noticed a lot of overlap

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:50.670
and a lot of lime blurring
between these femcels,

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:55.670
these modern femcels, the
coquette girls, and ballet-core.

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:57.750
I've done a whole video on ballet-core,

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:00.690
but for coquette, girls who partake in it

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:03.000
flock to frilly slip
dresses, hair ribbons,

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.270
thigh-high socks, Mary Janes,
tight fitting tank tops,

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:09.210
cropped cardigans, cheeky
slogans on baby tees,

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:11.940
and overall pastel colors
and lace trimmings.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.700
Popular aesthetic icons
shared by all three subgroups

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:15.990
include Lana Del Rey,

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:18.390
particularly at the
beginning of her career,

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:20.310
Nina Sayers from "Black Swan",

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:22.980
Angelina Jolie's character
in "Girl, Interrupted",

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:24.787
Jennifer from "Jennifer's Body",

00:13:24.787 --> 00:13:27.570
"Gone Girl", and of course
models and actresses

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:30.060
that have heroin chic
or waif-like body types,

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:32.610
including Lily Rose-Depp, young Kate Moss,

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:34.500
Bella Hadid, and Mia Goth.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:36.840
A lot of these girls
also refer to themselves

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:38.580
as girlbloggers,

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:42.150
AKA girly girls who blog
about being a girl's girl

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:45.750
who have usernames like
@diorwaif or @cocainebambi,

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:48.507
who post grainy GIFs
of blonde '90s models,

00:13:48.507 --> 00:13:51.360
Mui Mui ballet flats,
young Natalie Portman,

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.490
Lana Del Rey lyrics, Patrick Bateman,

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:57.120
and memes that hint out or
explicitly state mental illness.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:58.890
There's a lot of romanticization

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:01.230
about being pretty and mentally unstable,

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:03.570
but that being okay because
you're still pretty.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:07.170
And terminology like pilled, maxxing,

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:09.270
being in your whatever, whatever era,

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:11.550
and esoteric get tossed around.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.560
There's also very unique turn of phrasing

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:14.667
that these girls use.

00:14:14.667 --> 00:14:18.757
Biz Sherbert describes it in
their article on girlblogging.

00:14:18.757 --> 00:14:21.487
"I'm unstable in a 'Black
Swan', 'The Virgin Suicides',

00:14:21.487 --> 00:14:23.347
"nepotism baby, cool girl monologue,

00:14:23.347 --> 00:14:25.057
"Sylvia Plath, Brittany Murphy,

00:14:25.057 --> 00:14:27.157
"'Fatal Attraction' kind of way.

00:14:27.157 --> 00:14:29.227
"A girlblogger's frequent lines of texts

00:14:29.227 --> 00:14:31.027
"rub together into an inner monologue

00:14:31.027 --> 00:14:33.337
"that looms over all her posts.

00:14:33.337 --> 00:14:35.797
"This diary voice, psychologically charged

00:14:35.797 --> 00:14:38.497
"and often bitchy like the
writing on a bathroom stall,

00:14:38.497 --> 00:14:40.177
"is what separates the girlblogger

00:14:40.177 --> 00:14:41.677
"from an impersonal moodboarder

00:14:41.677 --> 00:14:44.051
"with a similar taste for girly things."

00:14:44.051 --> 00:14:46.468
(edgy music)

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:53.850
- [Man] You Can't keep
getting away with this.

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:56.670
- So just to recap on what was happening

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:59.760
on Tumblr around 2012 to 2015,

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:02.250
a popular subculture on
Tumblr revolved heavily

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.230
around Lana Del Rey GIFs and lyrics,

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:06.870
black and white photos with
sad captions overlaid on them.

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:09.720
and cigarette self-harm and drug motifs.

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:13.260
And who can forget the hashtag
#prettywhenyou cry selfies.

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:14.640
This was all part of a movement

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:17.460
now known as the sad girl era.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:20.017
Rosemary Kirton defined a sad girl for i-D

00:15:20.017 --> 00:15:22.357
"as one who listens to
better music than you

00:15:22.357 --> 00:15:23.677
"and might spend her alone time

00:15:23.677 --> 00:15:25.267
"watching French films from the '60s

00:15:25.267 --> 00:15:27.630
"or angsty TV shows from the '90s."

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:29.040
But even before the 2010s,

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:31.200
the sad girl was not a new phenomenon.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:32.970
Like think of "Romeo + Juliet",

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:34.950
think of Ophelia from "Hamlet",

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:36.330
think of "Jane Eyre".

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:38.970
And yes, there were
always shared aesthetics

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:41.790
and films and songs that
sad girls leaned onto

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:46.320
as Rayne puts it, "To chicly
signal one's mental illness,"

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:49.050
but I will say it wasn't as self-aware

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:52.470
or self-commodifying as
the girlbloggers are today.

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:54.870
Sad girls mostly posted
stream of conscious thoughts

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:56.910
into the void on Tumblr,

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:59.790
and our collective
interest in Arctic Monkeys

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:03.690
and Marina and the Diamonds
music felt more like coincidence

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:05.580
than a calculated attempt

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:08.010
to aestheticize our own identities.

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:10.380
At this time, journalists
were releasing think pieces

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:12.060
about how the sad girl aesthetic movement

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:14.640
romanticized clinical
depression and eating disorders,

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:17.640
which I definitely agree that it was.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:18.930
I don't think it was intentional.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:23.460
Like I don't think any movie
director or any songwriter

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:28.380
was explicitly trying to
de-legitimize mental illness

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.440
with their music, but
that's just what happened.

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:32.580
And it definitely didn't help

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:34.920
that being sad was considered cool,

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:36.900
and so a lot of teenage girls

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:40.440
who probably weren't actually battling

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:42.900
with serious mental illnesses

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.780
kind of induced traits
of those mental illnesses

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:48.960
within themselves as an attempt to fit in.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.010
The sad girl movement

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:52.080
wasn't just pervasive on Tumblr, though.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:54.540
They were brands that were
selling pins, tote bags,

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:57.960
and necklaces with
phrases like Sad Girl Club

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:59.520
and other slogans on them.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:01.477
Hannah May wrote for HelloGiggles,

00:17:01.477 --> 00:17:04.267
"Even more disturbing than teens
using depression to be cool

00:17:04.267 --> 00:17:06.697
"is the possibility of
companies marketing depression

00:17:06.697 --> 00:17:08.730
"to those teens to make a quick buck."

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:11.040
Granted, I think one of
the main issues these days

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:14.250
is how out of hand consumerism has gotten,

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:16.860
and consumerism is
unfortunately a byproduct

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:21.180
of making everything into
different aesthetics.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:22.920
I asked what people
thought about this topic

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:24.960
on my Instagram Stories a couple days ago,

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.697
and one person said, "I feel
like now more than ever,

00:17:27.697 --> 00:17:29.250
"these aesthetics are so intertwined

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:31.650
with consumerism/trend cycles."

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:33.547
Similarly, another person said,

00:17:33.547 --> 00:17:34.807
"We can let women be angry

00:17:34.807 --> 00:17:38.190
"without attaching an
aesthetic/a title to it."

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:39.877
And yet another person said,

00:17:39.877 --> 00:17:41.857
"I think it's really
fun to be in a community

00:17:41.857 --> 00:17:44.527
"with lots of other girls who
like the same things as me,

00:17:44.527 --> 00:17:46.297
"but it can also be really exclusionary

00:17:46.297 --> 00:17:48.367
"and breed a pick me-esque mindset

00:17:48.367 --> 00:17:52.117
"if you aren't as indie/enjoy
too many mainstream things

00:17:52.117 --> 00:17:54.540
"or even dress somewhat basic."

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:57.000
I think these are all very valid points.

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.520
I also think that they are points

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:02.280
that were valid back in the 2010s.

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:05.490
If anyone remembers, like,
the whole hipster trend--

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:06.480
- Just so you know,

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:09.960
a macchiato is a shot of espresso
with a little foam on top.

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:12.630
It's not a giant latte
slathered in whipped cream

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:14.640
with caramel squeezed all over it.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:16.530
- Hey, what band is this?

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:17.880
- You've never heard of it.

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:22.680
- To really qualify as a hipster,

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:26.430
it wasn't enough to just
like the same things

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.890
as everyone else or have
the same kind of ideology.

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:31.560
Like, you had to get the square glasses,

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:33.660
you had to wear the infinity scarves,

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:38.160
you had to have some mustache
motifs somewhere on your body

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:41.730
or else your friends were probably like,

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:43.440
are you really a hipster?

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:44.880
And God forbid if you wanna listen

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:46.980
to Justin Bieber every once in a while.

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:48.210
The difference between then and now

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:51.690
is obviously social media,
because rather than just having

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:55.560
to gain approval from your
local group of friends,

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:59.220
the internet is so global and
now there's so many people

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:02.730
who belong to this community
who you are connected with

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:05.910
and who you are constantly
seeking approval from,

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:09.930
not to mention the endless
content-focused moodboards

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:13.050
that give you new consumers' ideas

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:15.090
of how to gain people's approval.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:19.650
But the biggest problem I have
with commodifying sadness,

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:21.690
even back in like the 2010s,

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:24.780
is that it made wallowing in sadness cool

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:27.630
and getting help uncool.

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:31.410
Similarly, in the femcel
girlblogger subculture,

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:36.410
by making female hysteria
and mental illness aesthetic,

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:40.620
it discourages young women
from wanting to get help.

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:43.650
22-year-old TikTokker
Jules Johnson told Insider

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:46.350
that she finds the trend
nihilistic and hollow.

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.517
She said, "In the female
manipulator space,

00:19:48.517 --> 00:19:50.467
"there's a lot of focus
on getting back at men

00:19:50.467 --> 00:19:52.027
"when you could direct that energy

00:19:52.027 --> 00:19:53.850
"towards loving other women."

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:56.880
But anyways, I think the
Tumblr girl ideology redux

00:19:56.880 --> 00:19:59.430
was kind of inevitable,
considering the style itself

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:01.110
is getting a renewed appreciation

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:03.630
with the tag #2014tumblr

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:07.230
having over 215 million views on TikTok.

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.750
Femcel-core also champions
the thin body type,

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:11.310
which is being echoed in the mainstream

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:14.220
with low rise pants and skirts
coming back in full force,

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:16.470
and even Kim Kardashians
slimming down her booty

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.183
in the past several months.

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:20.850
- I think some of you guys are forgetting

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:22.300
what Kim K used to look like.

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:27.533
Look at the, look at the mass, okay?

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:33.300
This about 40 pounds ass gone.

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:35.970
- Because of how influential
the Kardashians are,

00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:40.440
and because they were kind of
the ones to popularize BBLs

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:43.680
in the past 10 years to begin with,

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:48.680
a lot of people feel like
them shrinking their asses

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:51.900
is a sign that the BBL era is over.

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:53.940
BBL, by the way, stands
for Brazilian butt lift,

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:56.850
which is a very dangerous procedure

00:20:56.850 --> 00:21:00.660
that shifts the fat in your
stomach into your booty

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:03.150
to give you a more curvaceous figure.

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:05.190
Even without the Kardashians' involvement,

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:10.190
it might just be the natural
course of body of type trends

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:12.810
that's dictating this shift in silhouette,

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:13.920
because as we all know,

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:16.520
very unfortunately throughout
the course of history,

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:21.273
women's silhouettes have been trends.

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:26.010
It's dipped from curvy to
skinny, to curvy to skinny,

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:29.220
and we've just been curvy
in the last 10 years

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:33.120
and I guess it's time
to go back to skinny.

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:37.110
Obviously I think BBLs and
kind of escaping that phase

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:40.080
is a good thing, because BBLs
are incredibly dangerous.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.240
Like you can literally
die on the operating table

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:45.420
for getting one, but I don't think

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:47.580
going the other end of
the spectrum is better,

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:51.090
because I grew up in the '90s and 2000s.

00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:53.760
Like, everyone had an eating disorder

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:57.720
because of how skinny
everyone in the media was.

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:00.150
- Do you know what really just kills me

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:01.500
about this whole thing

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:03.450
is the clothes that you are gonna get?

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:06.180
I mean, you don't deserve them.

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:09.146
You eat carbs, for Christ's sake.

00:22:09.146 --> 00:22:11.563
(edgy music)

00:22:15.810 --> 00:22:17.640
- Even though I think
it's somewhat natural

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:20.910
for girls to feel very angsty

00:22:20.910 --> 00:22:24.150
or to feel a certain way
about their place in society,

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:27.480
I also think that there have
been movements and trends

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:30.600
that have occurred in
the past five years or so

00:22:30.600 --> 00:22:35.600
that has also contributed
to the rise of femcel-core.

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:38.280
For example, during the first
two years of the pandemic,

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:41.580
there was a major societal
trend towards self-improvement.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:42.870
A lot of people felt pressured

00:22:42.870 --> 00:22:45.390
to utilize their time in
isolation productively,

00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:47.910
either by reading as
many books as possible,

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:50.580
listening to as many podcasts as possible,

00:22:50.580 --> 00:22:52.830
watching as many movies as possible,

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:54.780
learning new skills,

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:56.070
getting fit,

00:22:56.070 --> 00:22:57.930
dieting, you name it.

00:22:57.930 --> 00:23:00.060
The desire for self-improvement gave rise

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:01.200
to the that girl trend,

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:03.450
which was a major trend
on TikTok and Instagram

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:04.680
and revolved around girls

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:06.390
who essentially had their lives together,

00:23:06.390 --> 00:23:09.750
or at least looked like they
had their lives together.

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:13.230
These girls would film
themselves waking up at 5:00 AM,

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:16.770
going on runs, doing
Pilates in the morning,

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:21.770
drinking green smoothies,
wearing beige athleisure sets,

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:27.570
and meditating and journaling,

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:29.340
gratitude journaling regularly,

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:32.640
just doing everything that we feel

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:35.970
we are supposed to do for our
mental and physical health,

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:39.360
all while looking absolutely amazing.

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:42.720
While some people were inspired
by the whole that girl trend

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:45.090
to get their life together,

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:48.840
a lot of people also felt very uninspired

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:52.620
because they felt like the trend
was inaccessible for people

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:57.510
who were not white, skinny, or
at least upper middle class.

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:00.090
Laura Pitcher wrote an
article on that girl

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:01.230
for I-D last year,

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:03.907
noting that it was a rebranding
of girl boss culture.

00:24:03.907 --> 00:24:06.457
"Similar to other fitness
videos on social media,

00:24:06.457 --> 00:24:08.407
"being 'that girl' means
encompassing a life

00:24:08.407 --> 00:24:10.687
"based on mainstream notions of wellness.

00:24:10.687 --> 00:24:12.937
"However, there's bizarrely no end goal.

00:24:12.937 --> 00:24:14.407
"No marathon you train up to,

00:24:14.407 --> 00:24:16.237
"no reason to wake up at 5:00 AM,

00:24:16.237 --> 00:24:18.480
"other than that it seems more efficient."

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:21.300
And this is just a general
thing with our society,

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:24.960
like a general problem, where
a lot of our health goals

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:27.960
are focused on aesthetic motivations

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:29.940
rather than scientific ones.

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:32.820
For example, a lot of dieting books talk

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:34.650
about how to lose weight,

00:24:34.650 --> 00:24:38.340
but not why you should want to lose weight

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:42.420
because our dieting culture
revolves around looking thin,

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:46.410
not being healthier for
whatever your body type allows.

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:51.390
It actually is a healthy
thing for their body to do,

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:52.650
but just because they feel

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:55.830
like it's more aesthetically productive.

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:57.840
So because there's a lot of pressure

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:01.410
to constantly be improving yourself,

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:04.260
it makes sense that there
would inevitably be a movement

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:06.720
that pushes back against
that kind of rhetoric.

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:09.007
Jia Tolentino wrote in
her book "Trick Mirror",

00:25:09.007 --> 00:25:11.977
"The ideal woman has always
been conceptually overworked,

00:25:11.977 --> 00:25:14.857
"an inorganic thing
engineered to look natural.

00:25:14.857 --> 00:25:17.197
"Figuring out how to 'get
better' at being a woman

00:25:17.197 --> 00:25:20.460
"is a ridiculous and
often amoral project."

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:24.780
A lot of girls have taken this desire

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:28.080
to not be perfect to the extreme, though.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:31.200
Lanigan also wrote another
article earlier this year

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:33.030
talking about feral girl summer,

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:36.600
which explored the part satirical
but part very true desire

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:38.460
to just be imperfect.

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:41.460
The ultimate power of
being in your fleabag era

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:44.880
or being a femcel or
embracing a feral girl summer

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:47.550
is that you're allegedly self-aware.

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:49.770
You're aware that womanhood is oppressive,

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:54.060
and rather than trying to lean
in and hustle 'til you die,

00:25:54.060 --> 00:25:58.260
you've just decided to give
up, sit back, and be cynical.

00:25:58.260 --> 00:25:59.940
It's ironically kind of

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.700
I'm not like the other girls ideology,

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:06.930
despite its heavy reliance
on feminine aesthetics.

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:09.900
I don't think having a
little nihilistic era

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:13.860
every so often in your life
is a bad thing inherently,

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:17.490
but I think it can be
dangerous if it goes to extreme

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:19.140
or if it becomes so mainstream

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:22.560
that it takes up valuable
conversation space

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:26.730
for actually productive feminist action.

00:26:26.730 --> 00:26:28.087
As Hailey McNiff writes,

00:26:28.087 --> 00:26:30.277
"If women are drolly accepting their pain

00:26:30.277 --> 00:26:32.047
"as inborn and inevitable,

00:26:32.047 --> 00:26:34.087
"there may be an empowering
sense of numbness

00:26:34.087 --> 00:26:35.797
"in fully embracing that pain,

00:26:35.797 --> 00:26:37.597
"in running headlong into it yourself

00:26:37.597 --> 00:26:41.130
"and even bringing others down
with you if you so choose."

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:42.420
It's definitely frustrating,

00:26:42.420 --> 00:26:44.670
and one of the number one complaints

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:48.990
I got from my Instagram
Stories were women saying

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:53.990
that the movement is just not reasonable

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:57.960
for a woman of color or a
girl of color to partake in.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:00.450
Aurora Muir wrote a great article on this

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:03.270
and how disassociating
to the world's problems

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:06.060
keeps oppressors comfortably empowered.

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:09.037
She writes, "These white
dissociating feminists

00:27:09.037 --> 00:27:10.463
"are holding up their hands and saying,

00:27:10.463 --> 00:27:12.337
"'There is nothing I can do about it.

00:27:12.337 --> 00:27:14.767
"The world is just too
fucked up for me to help.

00:27:14.767 --> 00:27:16.980
"I'm gonna have to lie down.'"

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:20.527
She also offers the alternative
of community care and love.

00:27:20.527 --> 00:27:22.117
"Being a person is fucking hard

00:27:22.117 --> 00:27:23.827
"and it's fucking horrible sometimes.

00:27:23.827 --> 00:27:25.597
"But I'd like to give
our daughters and sisters

00:27:25.597 --> 00:27:27.247
"a world they want to be a part of,

00:27:27.247 --> 00:27:29.797
"where they can laugh and love
as opposed to one so bitter

00:27:29.797 --> 00:27:31.507
"they must wither themselves away

00:27:31.507 --> 00:27:34.350
"in an effort to spare
themselves from it."

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:38.250
I don't think all modern
femcels are bitter, by the way.

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:39.720
I think some of them have derived

00:27:39.720 --> 00:27:43.743
some kind of sick pleasure
in being stuck in the system,

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:47.670
kind of like a Stockholm syndrome,

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:49.860
but I do agree with Muir overall.

00:27:49.860 --> 00:27:52.260
I think a lot can be
said about how nihilism

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:55.050
can numb us from making any progress.

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:57.960
And rather than just flipping
the script on girl bosses

00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:02.250
and being like, girls don't
actually have to lean in

00:28:02.250 --> 00:28:05.280
and work super hard and be the best,

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:09.360
it's worth just having more
productive conversations

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:12.060
on how we can make girlhood and womanhood

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:14.610
more bearable and even enjoyable

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:16.740
without being self-destructive.

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:18.090
But you know, what do I know?

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:20.310
I was a teenage girl once, too.

00:28:20.310 --> 00:28:22.350
Okay, everyone, that's
all I have for today.

00:28:22.350 --> 00:28:23.850
Thank you so much for tuning in.

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:25.500
Please let me know in the
comments what you think

00:28:25.500 --> 00:28:29.280
about toxic femininity and all the things

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:31.380
that I've discussed today.

00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:32.760
I would love to hear your input.

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:34.260
I feel like it's one of those topics

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:38.460
that has become way more
in vogue to discuss about,

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:40.290
and therefore has piqued my interest

00:28:40.290 --> 00:28:43.980
because I keep seeing new and
interesting takes about it.

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:46.500
And I'm just like very
interested, I guess,

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:51.500
in women's studies
because I am one. (laughs)

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:53.280
Thank you for sticking around.

00:28:53.280 --> 00:28:54.900
I hope you have a lovely rest of your day

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:56.520
and I'll see you next time.

00:28:56.520 --> 00:28:57.757
Bye.

00:28:57.757 --> 00:28:58.590
(blows kiss)

